00:41:01,872 --> 00:41:03,676
Speaker 0: You open up Task Manager and you see it there.

549
00:41:03,776 --> 00:41:05,460
Speaker 0: Doom.exe, that is a process.

550
00:41:05,981 --> 00:41:14,540
Speaker 0: The operating system looks at all those processes, all the things in the Task Manager and the operating system says, all right, you get to use this core.

551
00:41:14,720 --> 00:41:15,263
Speaker 0: Go ahead.

552
00:41:15,444 --> 00:41:16,632
Speaker 0: All right, now you can stop.

553
00:41:16,712 --> 00:41:18,000
Speaker 0: Someone else is going to use the core now.

554
00:41:18,080 --> 00:41:28,360
Speaker 0: And it's scheduling which of the processes in the Task Manager or whatever your operating system has activity monitor and Mac OS or just top and Linux or whatever.

555
00:41:28,961 --> 00:41:34,935
Speaker 0: And the operating system is like, OK, we've got all these processes that want to do computing.

556
00:41:35,517 --> 00:41:36,900
Speaker 0: They got different priority levels.

557
00:41:37,140 --> 00:41:41,380
Speaker 0: Some of them are high priority, like the Doom that you're playing right now is really high priority.

558
00:41:41,881 --> 00:41:48,100
Speaker 0: And some things are low priority, like, you know, check your email in the background is lower priority.

559
00:41:49,121 --> 00:41:53,171
Speaker 0: And it decides which processes go to which CPUs when.

560
00:41:53,451 --> 00:41:54,574
Speaker 0: It's like, OK, you go now.

561
00:41:54,774 --> 00:41:55,316
Speaker 0: Now you go.

562
00:41:55,336 --> 00:41:56,398
Speaker 0: I'm going to stop you.

563
00:41:56,499 --> 00:41:56,980
Speaker 0: Now you go.

564
00:41:57,601 --> 00:42:11,220
Speaker 0: And so if you can write your program in such a way to where you can just have multiple processes, it's their programs that work that way and they don't need to sort of talk to each other.

