568
00:42:29,583 --> 00:42:39,360
Speaker 1: way back like many jobs ago when I was doing operations are basically our trading environment had like 14 different demons that would each spawn as their own process.

569
00:42:39,901 --> 00:42:52,700
Speaker 1: Now, they communicated with each other via shared memory, but each one could be allocated to like its own core or it's like it was an independent process that the operating system could schedule on its own as a unit.

570
00:42:52,901 --> 00:42:56,961
Speaker 1: And that worked very effectively in like the year 2007.

571
00:42:56,961 --> 00:42:59,564
Speaker 0: I mean, a good example would be like a Web server, right?

572
00:42:59,584 --> 00:43:01,447
Speaker 0: Let's say you're just serving up Web pages.

573
00:43:01,967 --> 00:43:11,220
Speaker 0: It's like, well, any individual HTTP request for a Web page, the response to that can be calculated independently of any other request that's coming in.

574
00:43:11,881 --> 00:43:17,840
Speaker 0: So if you have four cores, you might as well spin up four Web servers if you're not doing anything else with the computer.

575
00:43:18,342 --> 00:43:22,400
Speaker 0: And now you can handle four times as many requests as long as your network isn't jacked.

576
00:43:23,640 --> 00:43:30,600
Speaker 0: And now you've got four people independently processing Web requests for your Web server and they never need to talk to each other.

577
00:43:30,680 --> 00:43:35,660
Speaker 0: They don't care what the other three are doing because those requests are unrelated and it's all good.

578
00:43:36,880 --> 00:43:41,199
Speaker 0: So the question is, OK, well, what if they do need to talk to each other?

579
00:43:41,520 --> 00:43:41,801
Speaker 0: Right.

580
00:43:42,683 --> 00:43:44,306
Speaker 0: There's two ways to solve this problem.

581
00:43:44,507 --> 00:43:50,500
Speaker 0: One way is inter process communication, which is a programming challenge in its own right.

582
00:43:51,061 --> 00:44:01,700
Speaker 0: You can use things like named pipes or just files or any some kind of shared resource, because each process has its own memory.

583
00:44:01,961 --> 00:44:02,101
Speaker 0: Right.

584
00:44:02,141 --> 00:44:07,600
Speaker 0: The operating system, when you make a process, is like here is a piece of memory for your process to use.

585
00:44:08,021 --> 00:44:08,884
Speaker 0: It's all yours.

586
00:44:08,985 --> 00:44:11,373
Speaker 0: You can go ham in that area of the RAM.

587
00:44:11,433 --> 00:44:12,336
Speaker 0: It belongs to you.

588
00:44:12,677 --> 00:44:13,500
Speaker 0: You process you.

589
00:44:13,680 --> 00:44:21,300
Speaker 0: And I guarantee you no other processes are going to mess with that RAM unless the only way that RAM is getting messed with is if I come and destroy you.

590
00:44:23,341 --> 00:44:25,268
Speaker 0: And you can ask for more RAM if you need more.

591
00:44:25,328 --> 00:44:26,212
Speaker 0: And I might give you more.

592
00:44:26,352 --> 00:44:28,440
Speaker 0: I might not, depending if there is any available.

593
00:44:28,580 --> 00:44:30,646
Speaker 0: But that RAM is yours.

594
00:44:30,846 --> 00:44:36,200
Speaker 0: And so no other process can see what's going on in that RAM because that would be hack soaring.

595
00:44:38,621 --> 00:44:40,446
Speaker 0: So you spark four processes.

596
00:44:40,486 --> 00:44:45,160
Speaker 0: They all have their own RAM that they're all working with and they can't see what each other's are up to.

597
00:44:45,701 --> 00:44:47,784
Speaker 0: But they can talk through some other means.

598
00:44:47,805 --> 00:44:48,486
Speaker 0: Right.

599
00:44:48,726 --> 00:44:56,120
Speaker 0: Like a file that they all, you know, maybe one of them, for example, is like writing to a file and the other three are like reading from the file.

600
00:44:56,220 --> 00:45:02,740
Speaker 0: You could have a database, for example, where, you know, you have one database process that could do insert and select queries.

601
00:45:03,101 --> 00:45:08,340
Speaker 0: So you can update the data and three other database processes that only do reading queries and they don't do any writing.

602
00:45:08,781 --> 00:45:13,090
Speaker 0: There's no conflicts in terms of who writes in what order and causing any problems.

603
00:45:13,651 --> 00:45:17,940
Speaker 0: And now because that data is in a file, they can all share.

604
00:45:18,301 --> 00:45:19,744
Speaker 0: And you've got a multi-process setup.

605
00:45:19,784 --> 00:45:22,089
Speaker 0: that's improving your performance in some way.

606
00:45:22,891 --> 00:45:26,820
Speaker 0: But what if you want to go faster and you got something to share?

607
00:45:27,221 --> 00:45:27,461
Speaker 0: Right.

608
00:45:27,521 --> 00:45:35,200
Speaker 0: Well, in that case, you want to be able to share the RAM because RAM is way faster than other things and other means of communication.

609
00:45:36,081 --> 00:45:38,948
Speaker 0: And so how can you have processes that share the RAM?

610
00:45:38,968 --> 00:45:43,440
Speaker 0: I mean, very complex topic, but basically what you do is you make threads instead.

611
00:45:44,761 --> 00:45:50,300
Speaker 0: If you have one process, the process can have threads, which are like processes underneath a process.

612
00:45:51,162 --> 00:45:55,394
Speaker 0: And then those threads are all part of the one process.

613
00:45:55,554 --> 00:45:57,580
Speaker 0: So they all can see all the same RAM.

614
00:45:58,500 --> 00:46:06,180
Speaker 0: The downside is you now have to sort of maybe in some parts schedule or manage your own threads, right?

615
00:46:06,260 --> 00:46:13,260
Speaker 0: Because the operating system may or may not manage those to a certain extent, depending on a whole bunch of complicated nonsense.

616
00:46:15,182 --> 00:46:23,140
Speaker 0: But your process can just have a bunch of simultaneous threads and you could even put those on different cores so they truly happen simultaneously.

617
00:46:24,401 --> 00:46:32,597
Speaker 0: And they can share RAM and see the same RAM and write to the same RAM and read the same RAM, which can make things go really fast if that's necessary.

618
00:46:35,261 --> 00:46:39,533
Speaker 0: The downside is that you now have to program in a thread-safe manner.

619
00:46:39,573 --> 00:46:42,180
Speaker 0: This is what people mean when they say thread-safe, right?

620
00:46:42,500 --> 00:46:47,899
Speaker 0: Is to where the threads won't step on each other's toes and mess up the memory and screw everything up.

621
00:46:48,921 --> 00:46:54,060
Speaker 0: Because like, let's say one guy writes and the other guy overwrites it, and now it's all ruined.

622
00:46:54,720 --> 00:46:56,765
Speaker 0: Or one guy is trying to read it and the other, right?

623
00:47:04,741 --> 00:47:10,200
Speaker 0: In computer science college, they'll teach you about the dining philosophers and whatnot, right?

624
00:47:10,700 --> 00:47:12,003
Speaker 0: It's a huge topic.

625
00:47:12,023 --> 00:47:20,040
Speaker 0: There's tons of different algorithms you can use to basically guarantee that the threads aren't going to step on each other.

626
00:47:20,160 --> 00:47:24,778
Speaker 0: And they'll stop and wait for it to be safe to do something before they go and do it.

627
00:47:26,164 --> 00:47:27,839
Speaker 0: And otherwise, they'll do as much as they can.

628
00:47:28,581 --> 00:47:29,844
Speaker 1: God, I have a flashback.

629
00:47:29,884 --> 00:47:36,356
Speaker 1: So when I worked at that company, we had the most dreaded error that would pop up on my console.

630
00:47:36,416 --> 00:47:38,440
Speaker 1: that meant all hell is about to break loose.

631
00:47:38,801 --> 00:47:38,901
Speaker 1: Is?

632
00:47:38,921 --> 00:47:52,020
Speaker 1: we'd see spinlock errors, which usually were followed by a deadlock, meaning two threads within one of our processes basically got deadlocked against each other in some way and everything would crash.

633
00:47:54,021 --> 00:48:03,820
Speaker 1: And finding the causes of those issues was like the most senior developers at the company's sole job was to work on that kind of problem.

634
00:48:05,481 --> 00:48:14,600
Speaker 0: And so it's like, if you just do programming tutorials, you're not really going to be writing anything like this unless you know, you'll know if you are, right?

635
00:48:14,640 --> 00:48:16,364
Speaker 0: It's not like it happens in most programs.

636
00:48:16,404 --> 00:48:17,507
Speaker 0: You just write programs.

637
00:48:17,928 --> 00:48:22,780
Speaker 0: They're going to be single process, single threaded programs that just execute from beginning to end.

638
00:48:22,980 --> 00:48:26,919
Speaker 0: You're still multitasking because the operating system, your process is one among many.

639
00:48:28,182 --> 00:48:39,500
Speaker 0: But your program isn't going to have any of these concurrency issues unless you specifically start doing special code to be multiprocess or multithread within your one program.

640
00:48:41,562 --> 00:48:46,240
Speaker 0: And so back to the original, the news, the global interpreter lock in Python.

641
00:48:46,920 --> 00:48:49,005
Speaker 0: So Python is an interpreted language.

642
00:48:49,046 --> 00:48:50,008
Speaker 0: You have Python code.

643
00:48:50,409 --> 00:48:54,540
Speaker 0: The Python interpreter reads the code and executes it as it goes through the code.

644
00:48:54,821 --> 00:48:57,920
Speaker 0: It actually gets converted to byte code first, but that doesn't matter.

645
00:49:00,120 --> 00:49:15,690
Speaker 0: And so there is this global interpreter lock with any Python program where basically even if you write a multithreaded program, the interpreter is only going to be working on one at a time.

646
00:49:16,950 --> 00:49:22,242
Speaker 0: So you can still get bonuses to performance in Python with lots of threads.

647
00:49:22,783 --> 00:49:26,050
Speaker 0: if, for example, you have lots of IO happening, right?

648
00:49:26,090 --> 00:49:35,450
Speaker 0: Let's say you've got like, you know, you're waiting for the network or you're waiting for some hard drives or some other input output devices to do something.

649
00:49:36,352 --> 00:49:42,650
Speaker 0: That thread that's doing the IO, let's say you have a thread to do network stuff, is waiting for network packets, right?

650
00:49:43,230 --> 00:49:56,510
Speaker 0: It can sort of pause and the interpreter can go off and do some other stuff and then come back to it instead of just your whole program sitting in a locked waiting for the network to finish, right?

651
00:49:56,991 --> 00:50:00,779
Speaker 0: And so you could do that even with the infamous GIL, right?

652
00:50:00,839 --> 00:50:01,240
Speaker 0: It's not like.

653
00:50:01,320 --> 00:50:06,110
Speaker 0: threads in Python without, you know, with the GIL are worthless, which I think is what a lot of people misunderstand.

654
00:50:08,091 --> 00:50:19,190
Speaker 0: But if you are writing Python and you have a lot of CPU intensive work, let's say you're computing Fibonacci sequence or digits of pi or something, right?

655
00:50:19,731 --> 00:50:21,313
Speaker 0: And you're doing it in parallel.

656
00:50:21,333 --> 00:50:33,330
Speaker 0: And it's all written in Python because you could actually, you know, write some C or some other language for a part of the program and Python would just talk to that and then you avoid the situation entirely.

657
00:50:35,310 --> 00:50:38,219
Speaker 0: Then the interpreter can only really do one of those at a time.

658
00:50:39,202 --> 00:50:41,670
Speaker 0: And so that's when the GIL is going to cause you problems.

659
00:50:41,810 --> 00:50:55,110
Speaker 0: And that's when getting rid of the GIL with the new experimental feature will help because now you could have basically the multiple different Python parts being interpreted and executed in parallel on different cores simultaneously.

660
00:50:57,099 --> 00:50:57,567
Speaker 0: And there you go.

661
00:50:58,150 --> 00:51:05,030
Speaker 1: And broadly, like the most computers, like browsers, things that you use every day, they do use this heavily.

662
00:51:05,170 --> 00:51:08,210
Speaker 1: Like right now my computer actually got every tab is like separate.

663
00:51:08,370 --> 00:51:16,190
Speaker 0: And then even when you're rendering a Web page, it's like all the different parts of the Web page, all different steps of loading a Web page can sort of be done separately.

664
00:51:16,370 --> 00:51:23,090
Speaker 0: It's like, well, you go off and get the CSS, you go download the images, you draw the images as they come in, you render the fonts.

665
00:51:23,250 --> 00:51:24,113
Speaker 0: It's like each.

666
00:51:24,274 --> 00:51:28,790
Speaker 0: all of that work can be done very separately in lots of different orders.

667
00:51:28,910 --> 00:51:31,109
Speaker 0: The order doesn't matter as long as the end result is the same.

668
00:51:31,711 --> 00:51:40,410
Speaker 0: And so you can get a lot of work done by having lots of threads and have each tab in like its own thread or process separately.

669
00:51:41,784 --> 00:51:41,910
Speaker 1: Yeah.

